.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt SETFSUID 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm setfsuid ,
.Nm setfsgid ,
.Nm setfsuid32 ,
.Nm setfsgid32
.Nd set the user and group IDs used for file access
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/fsuid.h
.Ft int
.Fn setfsuid "uid_t fsuid"
.Ft int
.Fn setfsgid "gid_t fsgid"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each thread has a file-system user ID and a file-system group ID beside
its real, effective and saved IDs.
They are the IDs every check of access to a file goes by: the
permission bits at an open, a search of a directory, an exec, the owner
tests of
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr chown 2
and
.Xr utimensat 2 ,
the sticky rule of a removal, and the owner and group a new file, FIFO,
socket, message queue or pseudo-terminal gets.
.Xr access 2
alone judges by the real IDs.
Signals, the System V IPC objects and every other check go by the
effective IDs.
.Pp
A file-system ID is the effective ID unless one of these calls set it
apart.
Every call of
.Xr setuid 2
that sets user IDs makes the file-system user ID the effective one again,
whether or not it changed the effective ID, and so does an exec; the
calls that set group IDs do the same for the file-system group ID.
A child of
.Xr fork 2
has its parent's.
.Pp
.Fn setfsuid
makes
.Fa fsuid
the calling thread's file-system user ID when it is the thread's real,
effective, saved or present file-system user ID, or the thread is
privileged
.Pq see Xr setuid 2 .
.Fn setfsgid
does the same with
.Fa fsgid
and the group IDs.
A call that changes an ID clears the process's dumpable flag and its
parent-death signal, as a change of an effective ID does; see
.Xr prctl 2 .
.Pp
The capabilities that Linux checks for file access,
.Dv CAP_CHOWN ,
.Dv CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE ,
.Dv CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH ,
.Dv CAP_FOWNER ,
.Dv CAP_FSETID ,
.Dv CAP_LINUX_IMMUTABLE ,
.Dv CAP_MKNOD
and
.Dv CAP_MAC_OVERRIDE ,
go by the file-system user ID: a thread whose file-system user ID leaves
0 loses them from its effective set, though its effective user ID stays
0, and one whose file-system user ID returns to 0 has again those it has
not given up.
A thread without them passes a file's permission bits, acts as a file's
owner, keeps set-ID bits across a write, makes a device node and changes
the immutable and append-only flags only as any other user may.
.Xr capget 2
and the CapEff line of
.Pa /proc/PID/status
show the set, and the fourth columns of that file's Uid and Gid lines
the two IDs.
.Ss System call numbers
On i386 musl calls
.Nm setfsuid32
(215) and
.Nm setfsgid32
(216), which take 32-bit IDs.
i386's
.Nm setfsuid
(138) and
.Nm setfsgid
(139) take a 16-bit ID, of which 0xFFFF is -1, and return the previous ID
whole, as Linux's do.
The i686 kernel has all four, and so has the x86-64 kernel for a program
that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
which
.Xr ia32 7
describes.
On x86-64 the calls are
.Nm setfsuid
(122) and
.Nm setfsgid
(123), with 32-bit IDs.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Both calls return the previous file-system ID, as the caller's user
namespace sees it, whether or not they changed it, and never an error.
An ID the caller may not take, or one its user namespace does not map,
-1 among them, changes nothing, so a call with -1 reads the present ID.
A caller that must know whether a change took place calls again with -1
and compares.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/fsidprobe.c
sets the file-system IDs to 1000 as root, creates a file and reads its
owner and group, checks that a file of mode 0600 owned by root no longer
opens while
.Xr access 2
still allows it, reads the fourth columns of
.Pa /proc/self/status
and the CapEff line with the file-system user ID at 1000 and back at 0,
and checks that
.Xr setuid 2
makes the file-system user ID the effective one again.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr access 2 ,
.Xr capget 2 ,
.Xr getuid 2 ,
.Xr setuid 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
.Sh STANDARDS
The calls are Linux's, and their rules are those of Linux 6.12.
The permitted set that
.Xr capget 2
reports is the capabilities the effective user ID gives and those of the
effective set; Linux keeps it apart.
